SM(516,{"n":"Romans 9","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 9.mp3","d":2711.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.48,"Okay. So now we have a little bit of a transition at the beginning of chapter nine."],[28.96,"Paul is sort of moving to a new topic that he introduces here."],[33.44,"He says, I tell the truth in Christ, I am not lying."],[36.24,"My conscience also bearing with me witness in the Holy Spirit."],[40.56,"That I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart."],[43.68,"So he invokes two witnesses."],[46.08,"He's got his conscience, he's got the Holy Spirit bearing witnesses."],[49.44,"This is true."],[50.96,"That he has this grief and sorrow in his heart."],[56.0,"For I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ, for my brethren, my countrymen, according to the flesh, who are Israelites, to whom pertain the adoption, the glory, the covenants, the giving of the law, the service of God, and the promises, of whom are the fathers, and from whom, according to the flesh, Christ came, who is overall the eternally blessed God."],[79.24,"Amen. So this is one of the examples."],[87.16,"The other one being Moses, who says something similar of the purest love that a human being can have."],[92.36,"And that's that St."],[93.48,"Paul says he would be willing to face eternal condemnation and be cut off from Christ if it meant that his fellow ethnic ethnic Jews, his fellow Judeans, could find salvation in Christ."],[113.6,"Moses at one point says the same thing about the people of Israel when God is angry at them."],[118.88,"He says, asks that he himself be blotted out of God's book of life, but that God not of this early them."],[127.68,"Is it clear to Paul that very few Israelites are following Christ?"],[135.52,"At this point, yes."],[136.96,"Because remember Saint Paul himself was out there with orders from the high priest going from town to town killing Christians."],[147.68,"So they're actively persecuting them."],[149.36,"And then after him embracing Jesus as the Messiah as he went to preach the gospel, remember there were groups of non-Christian Jews following him from city to city trying to get him stoned to death and trying to get him killed."],[164.6,"So not only is there a large segment of the Jewish community that hasn't embraced Jesus as the Messiah, this there's a large segment that's openly hostile to the Christians, to the brethren, sort of everywhere that he goes."],[179.64,"And so, but St."],[181.24,"Paul's response to that is not to hate them back or to curse them or to say, you know, well, they're all gonna go to hell, you know, for having rejected Christ, but it's to love them so much that he says, I'd be willing to go to to hell if they could find, if they could come the knowledge of the truth and embrace Christ."],[202.24,"Um that's why it's one of those the purest examples of of love, sort of parallel to the love of Christ."],[209.94,"Loving and forgiving the people who are murdering him."],[212.98,"You know. And so he talks about all the things, all the things that the people were given, sort of recounting those things from the Old Testament."],[226.26,"Sounds like all these things that they were given, all these things."],[232.26,"And, you know, not only all those things that culminates with, you know, Jesus himself is their Messiah."],[239.06,"He's the son of David"],[241.28,"He's the Jewish Messiah."],[246.08,"And so he came to them first."],[250.1,"Throughout his ministry, Christ said over and over, I came to the lost sheep of the tribes of Israel."],[255.72,"And the going out to the Gentiles came later."],[259.14,"After Christ had come to them and they had rejected him."],[267.3,"Paul really clearly identifies Christ as God."],[272.98,"For anybody who might want to think otherwise."],[279.62,"So what St."],[281.14,"Paul is doing here ising introduc a problem."],[282.18,"There's a problem"],[286.86,"If Jesus is really the Messiah."],[290.16,"If he's really the son of David who's come, this is how God has visited his people to save them."],[295.36,"This is the promise."],[297.84,"Why have so many of the Jewish people rejected him and show so much hatred towards him?"],[305.29,"That seems to make no sense."],[306.97,"You would think they would have received him joyfully."],[310.65,"And then maybe some of the Gentiles too."],[313.87,"But instead, what's happened is this huge body of the Jewish people have rejected him, actively hate him."],[322.11,"And then these weirdo pagans who who didn't receive all these things, who didn't receive the Torah and the Old Testament, who didn't have the priesthood and the temple and the sacrifices, who didn't have the prophets, who didn't have all these things, they've embraced Jesus as the Messiah."],[341.03,"So this is weird."],[342.47,"This is a problem."],[343.67,"How has this happened?"],[345.27,"That's what St."],[346.23,"Paul is now going to address."],[348.71,"Okay. So that's the topic he's introducing."],[352.31,"How did this happen?"],[353.27,"And and not just how did it happen, but why?"],[357.09,"Why would something like this happen?"],[362.13,"So chapter 9, verse 6."],[363.97,"But it is not that the word of God has taken no effect, for they are not all Israel who are of Israel, nor are they all children because they are the seed of Abraham."],[369.73,"But in Isaac your seed shall be called"],[383.29,"Does this mean all those things that God did?"],[386.03,"Giving the Torah, sending the prophets, all of that that came to naught."],[390.61,"That came to nothing."],[391.65,"Even Jesus' ministry coming as it was that came to nothing because the people didn't didn't believe?"],[397.66,"That's obviously a problem."],[399.98,"He says, No, that's not it."],[401.58,"He says, but not all in Israel are of Israel."],[406.12,"Meaning, and then he further defines that."],[408.44,"He says, not everyone who's descended from Abraham biologically."],[416.14,"Was part of God's covenant people."],[419.34,"From the very beginning."],[420.54,"That's why he goes to Isaac."],[422.14,"Through Isaac's shall your seed be reckoned."],[425.56,"So this isn't something new that happened, where it used to be everyone descended from Abraham was part of the covenant and was God's elect, and every everything was fine, and then Jesus came and now all of a sudden."],[440.26,"Everything's changed, and all these people are cut off."],[443.54,"Paul's saying this is always this has always been the case"],[444.14,"St. There have always been faithful and unfaithful in Israel."],[452.58,"And there have been a lot of times where the unfaithful have greatly out.numbered the faithful in the history of Israel, if you read the Old Testament"],[465.42,"So he says, that is those who are the children of the flesh, these are not the children of God, but the children of the promise are counted as the seed."],[474.14,"For this is the word of promise."],[475.9,"At this time I will come, and Sarah shall have a son."],[484.22,"Ishmael. Ishmael was not the one who God promised."],[491.06,"Okay. And so, the seed was reckoned through one."],[496.19,"That seed is leading up to Jesus, as he's just said."],[499.81,"Now, does this mean Ishmael isn't quote unquote saved?"],[507.01,"No. Ishmael was wicked."],[509.49,"Ishmael went to hell."],[512.63,"Not at all."],[513.75,"All I have to do is go back to Genesis."],[516.63,"But read it."],[517.11,"He was blessed by God and made numerous."],[519.67,"Remember, the promise to Abraham was that nation's plural would come from his loins."],[526.01,"But that the line of promise has always been reckoned through one branch."],[532.81,"And then there have been faithful and unfaithful."],[537.85,"Okay. Because remember, he's not just talking about the Jews now."],[544.53,"He's talking about the fact that there are faithless Jews, but also the fact that there are faithful Gentiles."],[554.13,"And so he's keeping both of those in view."],[556.95,"So if Ishmael is just a bad guy because he's not a Jew."],[560.8,"That doesn't really work for what St."],[563.68,"Paul is talking about."],[568.4,"And not only this, but when Rebecca also had conceived by one man, even by our father Isaac, for the children not yet being born, nor having done any good or evil, that the purpose of God according to election might stand, not of works, but of him who calls, it was said to her, the older shall serve the younger, as it is written, Jacob I have loved, but Esau I have hated."],[588.4,"Okay This is another verse that the Calvinists jump up and down."],[599.45,"To say, look, before they're born."],[602.79,"Before they've done any works, before they've done everything, anything, God loves Jacob and he hates Esau."],[609.85,"And the the older will serve the younger."],[612.65,"Okay. Now, that understanding works unless you've read the book of Genesis."],[621.13,"Okay And you've actually read the story of Jacob and Esau."],[626.61,"Because, first of all, Jacob I have loved, Esau I have hated, is not a quote from Genesis."],[635.33,"It's a quote from much, much later in the prophets."],[641.01,"It's from Malachi 1, verses 2 and 3."],[646.37,"Much, much later."],[647.49,"Yeah. After the exile."],[650.21,"Okay. And we'll get into what it's talking about in just a minute."],[654.93,"The quote from Genesis is St."],[656.61,"Paul's first quote the older shall serve the younger."],[660.95,"And what's that referring to?"],[662.55,"Well, Jacob and Esau were twins."],[665.44,"And Esau came out a couple of seconds first."],[670.54,"Which made Esau the firstborn."],[675.56,"And Jacob the secondborn."],[677.98,"Which would have meant Esau would have been the one who inherited the promises that had come down from Abraham, meaning what?"],[685.58,"Meaning, first of all, the land."],[689.55,"And uh the land of Canaan and the promise to be his God, and then he would have distributed that to his brother and to any other future brothers."],[701.03,"Because that's how firstborn status works."],[704.52,"But instead, God, because he had a purpose in mind."],[711.17,"Chose Jacob, later named Israel, to be the firstborn. of the two"],[719.57,"And that happens because Esau, being someone who uh followed the flesh rather than the spirit, came back and was super hungry one day, and Jacob offered to trade him a bowl of his thu that he had made for his birthright for the firstborn status."],[741.27,"Well, this shows us a couple things about Esau."],[743.11,"Number one, he falls into this category from chapter 8 of someone following the flesh and its desires."],[748.13,"He's just hungry, and I need food and all anything you get."],[751.49,"But it also shows his faithlessness."],[753.94,"Because if he really believed God's promises, that he was going to inherit this huge piece of land."],[760.18,"No Would he have sold that for a bowl of soup?"],[767.05,"No one in their right mind would, which means he didn't really believe it."],[771.53,"He didn't really value it."],[773.55,"He didn't have he wasn't faithful."],[775.31,"He didn't have faith."],[776.85,"He didn't believe."],[778.13,"And so because of that, he gives up his firstborn status."],[782.22,"And then there's sort of the trickery where he gets the blessing from Jacob gets the blessing from Isaac."],[787.48,"But then Isaac also blesses Esau."],[791.0,"So in our Calvinist interpretation, Esau is evil, and Esau goes to hell."],[797.88,"But is that what we see in the book of Genesis?"],[801.48,"The exact opposite."],[802.44,"At the end of Jacob's life, after he's gone and had all his sort of wily trickster adventures."],[808.21,"He's gonna meet it run into Esau again."],[811.01,"And he thinks, oh man, Esau's gonna kill me."],[814.07,"When he sees me, he is gonna kill me because I stole his, I swiped his birthright, I swiped the blessing, I've been up to all this chicanery and no good."],[822.81,"So he says, Here's what I'll do."],[825.05,"I'm gonna see Esau."],[826.73,"So what I'll do is I'll take my wives and my servants and my kids, and I'll give them gifts for Esau, and I'll send them all ahead of me."],[836.97,"So by the time I run into Esau, he will have seen all of these family members who are dependent on me and love me, and he will have received all these gifts, and so then maybe he won't kill me."],[846.25,"So we'll butter him up"],[850.65,"And we'll make him feel like a real heel if he kills me."],[852.97,"Because look at all these, look at all these cute kids I got."],[855.21,"You don't want to kill me."],[856.63,"They won't have a dad."],[858.17,"So he does all this."],[859.53,"And when he finally gets to Esau, what does Esau do?"],[862.89,"He throws his arms around him and hugs him and weeps because he's so happy to see him."],[868.09,"Because he forgives him and he loves him."],[871.05,"This is not a faithless, wicked person."],[875.71,"That's not how the Bible presents him as a person."],[880.15,"Okay? But he's not the firstborn."],[883.31,"Okay The uh what about the people descended from him?"],[890.07,"Because there's a nation descended from him, Edom."],[892.65,"The Edomites. And that's actually who the Malachi quote is talking about, the Edomites much later."],[898.17,"So the Edomites were just right off the bat bad people."],[901.9,"No. If you go to Deuteronomy chapter 2, and for time's sake, I won't go there and read it all now, but if you go to Deuteronomy chapter 2, when the people of Israel are on their way to the land, they've been freed from Egypt, they've wandered in the wilderness for 40 years."],[917.66,"Now they're going to come into the land of Canaan."],[919.58,"They come up to Edom, because it's in the south part."],[922.86,"And the land of Edom, by the way, is part of the land that God promised to Abraham."],[927.74,"The small part of it, but it's part of it."],[930.14,"And God says, you're not to take so much as one foot of land from the Edomites."],[932.62,"He says, Because they're your brothers"],[940.34,"He says, I brought them to this land and gave it to them."],[944.02,"I drove out the giant clans, the guys who are here, and gave them the land."],[951.78,"Just as I'm about to give you, to give you Canaan."],[955.78,"So they're your brothers."],[956.82,"You can trade with them."],[959.2,"You can visit with them, but you're not to take up arms against them, and you're not to take a foot of their land because it's not yours."],[965.28,"I gave it to them."],[966.88,"Okay. So they've inherited."],[969.7,"Not the prime inheritance, but they've received part of Abraham's inheritance."],[974.68,"God's brought them to the land and driven out the former residents in front of them, just as the people of Canaan, and they're brothers."],[976.12,"and So they're not wicked evil yet."],[984.96,"Well, maybe they got wicked and evil real quick after that."],[988.8,"Oh, well, one more thing to add."],[992.72,"Before that. When Moses flees Egypt, Moses murders the Egyptian, goes on the lamb."],[1000.82,"He runs. Who does he go and stay with?"],[1004.26,"Midianites. The Midianites."],[1006.63,"Who are descended from Esau's grandson Midian."],[1013.03,"So the Midianite tribes are made up of mostly Edomites and a few Ishmaelites."],[1019.59,"Who is Moses' father-in-law"],[1023.39,"Jethro. Jethro, who is and a priest of God most high, camped next to the mountain of God, which is where Moses first meets and learns the name Yahweh, who's already being worshiped by the Edomites."],[1048.67,"The whole time they were in Egypt, the descendants of Ishmael and Esau were out there in the desert worshiping Yahweh, the God of Israel, the God of their fathers, Abraham, Isaac, and Esau."],[1065.67,"In fact, archaeologically, the earliest references to the name Yahweh we've found are from the 11th and 12th century BC in Egyptian documents, who refer to him as the god of the Midianite tribes."],[1081.91,"Uh-huh. Out in the desert."],[1085.59,"There's no mention of Israel whatsoever."],[1089.99,"He's the god of those Midianite tribes."],[1093.75,"So they have Abamakar haven't fallen away."],[1096.39,"According to the Septuagint, according to the, well, I should say, according to the Greek Old Testament, at the end of the book of Job, it identifies Job as Jobab, Esau's great-grandson."],[1114.03,"So the Edomites in the Torah, not wicked."],[1120.29,"Not evil. They're your brothers."],[1124.05,"Now, Malachi, why, when we get to Malachi at the end of the exile, do the Edomit are the Edomites hated?"],[1132.21,"Why are they condemned?"],[1133.97,"Well, if you read Obadiah, which was all directed at the Edomites, and you read in the context in Malachi where this quote is from, at the time that Judah was being taken into exile by the Babylonians, the Edomites not only refused to help them against the Babylonians, but refused to help the refugees."],[1154.21,"In fact, they were robbing people"],[1157.45,"They were robbing Israelites or Judahites as they were being taken into taken into exile, mocking them, scoffing at them, using it as an opportunity to take territory in Judah to move into their territory."],[1170.89,"And so they're condemned for having mocked, Jiredat, looked down upon, victimized their brothers in Judah."],[1181.13,"And then did they re did they resist those who returned as well?"],[1185.93,"Yeah. They weren't friendly to those who returned."],[1189.33,"Um, but one of the most one of the most famous Edomites in the Bible, most people don't realize it, is an Edomite, uh because Edomite is the origin of Idumean."],[1199.21,"I knew that"],[1199.65,"That's what Herod was."],[1201.25,"Yeah, uh-huh. So"],[1209.18,"So that's something that happens much later."],[1211.9,"Okay. So the examples of Ishmael and Esau that St."],[1216.62,"Paul is using here are not examples of these are faithful, quote unquote, saved people, and these are wicked, quote unquote, unsaved people."],[1226.43,"He's talking about the fact that there's one line through which the inheritance is reckoned, through which the firstborn is reckoned."],[1237.32,"And where does that firstborn status end up?"],[1241.8,"What have we just been reading in chapter 8?"],[1245.88,"Jesus. There's always been one firstborn."],[1251.8,"And now that firstborn is Jesus."],[1254.76,"And so if you want to be an heir of the promises, you have to be a co-heir with Christ."],[1262.92,"Just as if you wanted to be an heir of the promises, you had to be a co heir with Isaac."],[1267.08,"You had to be a co heir with Jacob."],[1271.29,"Now Christ is the firstborn par excellence."],[1275.43,"Who inherits all creation?"],[1277.11,"If you want to be an heir, you have to receive it in Christ."],[1283.31,"And so if these people, though they're descended from Abraham, reject Christ."],[1293.05,"Reject the firstborn, they're not going to inherit."],[1299.37,"They're not going to inherit apart from him."],[1302.73,"And you see that with the two quotes he gives with the Edomites."],[1307.19,"The Edomites early on were co-heirs."],[1312.31,"They were fellow inheritors because of their relationship with Israel and then Judah."],[1317.91,"But then when they turned against, when they turned against Judah."],[1325.01,"We're in enmity with Judah, they lost their inheritance."],[1329.33,"They were cut off, even though they were still biologically descended from Abraham"],[1338.17,"And so this is the same thing."],[1340.01,"That's how these people have now been cut off through their faithfulness."],[1343.93,"By rejecting Christ, they're now cut off from the inheritance that was theirs originally."],[1354.01,"But that they can only receive as co-heirs with Christ the firstborn."],[1360.29,"So he's talking about not about salvation, but about firstborn status."],[1374.61,"So verse 14, what shall we say then?"],[1376.69,"Is there unrighteousness with God?"],[1379.33,"Certainly not. Okay."],[1382.61,"Meaning, didn't God make these promises to these people, and now they're not going to inherit them."],[1390.13,"So is God unrighteous?"],[1391.41,"Does God not keep his promises?"],[1394.37,"Certainly not. Certainly not."],[1397.94,"That's actually what's translated here is certainly not, is uh a Greek phrase."],[1403.46,"Um that's in a weird, it's a weird verb tense."],[1406.9,"It's called the optative or a weird mood."],[1409.06,"It's called the optative."],[1410.58,"Uh it's only used sixty-four times in the Bible."],[1413.86,"And uh almost all of them, like 80, eighty-five percent of them, are St."],[1419.06,"Paul saying this here, certainly not, or may it not be."],[1419.3,"'s It something along those lines."],[1424.14,"May it never happen."],[1429.5,"So this is sort of part of his, this is one of his phrases."],[1435.26,"You know, there's certain yeah, there's certain people when you listen to them as a teacher, you know, yeah."],[1439.74,"You have sort of verbal ticks, sort of phrases you use on the this is one of this is one of St."],[1445.74,"Paul's uh it's me guinnito."],[1450.46,"Um for he says to Moses, I will have mercy on whomever I will have mercy, and I will have compassion on whomever I will have compassion."],[1459.5,"So then it is not of him who wills nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy."],[1466.18,"So God said to Moses, I'm under no obligation."],[1473.08,"This is accusing God of unrighteousness, saying he didn't meet some obligation."],[1477.46,"Like he once he made these promises to Abraham into a seed."],[1480.5,"Well now he's obligated, to bring to salvation all of Abraham's."],[1486.4,"No. He said to Moses, I, you know, I'll have compassion on whom I have compassion and show mercy."],[1492.26,"He's under no obligation to do any of this for anyone."],[1496.83,"He does this freely out of out of love."],[1500.65,"And so when he says, it's not him who wills or him who runs, this is not something we earn."],[1506.59,"There's not a way for us to do something that then obligates God to give us salvation and give us this inheritance."],[1516.53,"Well, I met requirements ABC."],[1518.95,"I'm descended from Abraham."],[1520.39,"I got circumcised."],[1522.16,"I wore the tassels on my garments."],[1524.16,"I didn't eat any pork, so you now have to be an inheritance of the breath."],[1531.2,"No. No. For the scripture says to the Pharaoh, For this very purpose I have raised you up, that I may show my power in you, and that my name may be declared in all the earth."],[1545.54,"Therefore, he has mercy on whom he wills, and whom. he wills he hardens"],[1552.3,"So he's saying about Pharaoh."],[1555.39,"Referring to this hardening."],[1588.03,"Sometimes it says just Pharaoh's heart was hardened."],[1591.71,"Sometimes it says Pharaoh hardened his heart."],[1594.47,"Sometimes it says God hardened Pharaoh's heart."],[1598.63,"They're just sort of mixed together with no real no real rhyme or reason."],[1603.53,"In terms of you know how exactly it's working in each in each case."],[1609.93,"Okay. Um but what St."],[1612.97,"Paul is pointing to here is is a particular explanation of that, which is that God is using Pharaoh to accomplish something in the plagues."],[1626.11,"That uh Pharaoh, because of who he was and the things he had done."],[1631.75,"Specifically, the thing that really sets this off at the beginning of Exodus in Exodus chapter 1, is that the people of Israel have begun, it says it's usually translated grown numerous or have been fruitful."],[1646.07,"But all of this language is language that's picked up from Genesis."],[1650.15,"Because the instruction that God gives to Adam and Eve after they're created, and then again after they're expelled from paradise, and then again to Noah after the flood is what?"],[1659.99,"Be fruitful and multiply and fill the same word that's translated teeming in the King James Version in Genesis 1 about the birds of the air and the fish of the sea, that the sky and the sea are teeming with life."],[1678.04,"They're just full of life."],[1679.88,"Because this is what God does, He brings life."],[1683.36,"And so at the beginning of Exodus, Israel's doing that."],[1686.94,"They're being fruitful and multiplying, teeming with life."],[1689.9,"And Pharaoh says, We've got to put a stop to this."],[1693.18,"Because pretty soon there's going to be more of them than there are of us."],[1696.86,"They're going to try and take my power."],[1699.98,"And so what does he do?"],[1701.9,"First, he tells all the midwives who do uh the Egyptian equivalent of prenatal exams."],[1709.02,"And the Egyptians, without getting into graphic detail, had ways of telling whether a fetus was male or female."],[1716.78,"Okay. And so what is what he literally tells them is that when they go to the Israelite women, they need to determine whether the baby is male or female."],[1725.9,"'s And if it male, they need to abort it."],[1726.02,"It's still in the womb."],[1730.18,"What it literally says in Exodus 1 is when you see the infant upon the potter's wheel."],[1736.2,"Because in Egyptian religion, there's a god Canum who has a ram's head, and he forms human beings on a potter's wheel."],[1744.52,"So he says, When you see the baby on the potter's wheel, you see the baby forming in the womb."],[1748.52,"You determine if it's a male, if it's a male, you abort it."],[1751.72,"You kill it."],[1753.24,"And so the midwives come back to the midwives don't want to do this."],[1756.68,"And so the excuse they get when they come back to Pharaoh is these Israelite women are really hardy women."],[1763.0,"They don't call us to do prenatal exams."],[1765.58,"And by the time we hear that they're pregnant, we show up, they've already had the baby."],[1769.28,"Because they're tough."],[1769.84,"ugh. They just woman up and go thro it"],[1774.36,"And so then what does Pharaoh say?"],[1775.8,"Pharaoh says, okay, anyone who sees a male Israelite baby, throw it in the river and drown it."],[1781.41,"So he's massacred, you know, untold thousands of Israelites."],[1787.09,"Okay, killed these children."],[1789.17,"And so this is why all the plagues build up to the Passover and the death of the firstborn of Egypt, because God is balancing the scales."],[1801.73,"He's making things right"],[1803.85,"And he's making a display to everyone that the gods of Egypt don't have any power to withstand him."],[1810.65,"He says several times during the process that he's executing judgment against the gods of Egypt."],[1815.77,"Of which, remember, Pharaoh considered himself to be one."],[1820.73,"He considered himself to be a god."],[1822.81,"So he shows conclusively they don't have the power of life and death."],[1827.13,"They can't stand before him."],[1828.65,"And this display is made not only to them, but because Egypt was the most powerful nation in the world at the time, it was made to everybody."],[1836.65,"When Joshua and Caleb, the spies, show up in Canaan and show up with Rahab, Rahab says, Yeah, we heard about what your God did to the Egyptians."],[1845.69,"And we don't want no part of that."],[1847.13,"He's he's God for sure."],[1849.67,"Like, you know, we don't."],[1852.48,"And so for that to happen, for God to accomplish that purpose, for him to establish justice for what Pharaoh Pharaoh had done and all this, couldn't chicken out halfway through the plagues."],[1865.4,"As weak as he was."],[1867.17,"He couldn't chicken out halfway."],[1868.61,"He was in for a penny, in for a pound."],[1871.71,"And so this is what St."],[1873.39,"Paul is pointing to."],[1874.99,"For God to accomplish his his end goal, his end purpose."],[1879.17,"Pharaoh's heart had to be hardened for a time."],[1882.77,"Okay. Now, why is this important?"],[1885.01,"Because he's making this comparison."],[1888.77,"As this discussion continues, he's going to make it more explicitly."],[1891.57,"He's making this comparison to the Jewish people who have so far not believed, have not accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[1904.01,"He's saying that this isn't accidental."],[1908.01,"God has a purpose in this."],[1923.37,"But he's saying now already, he's dropping the dime that God has a purpose in this."],[1930.57,"In them not accepting Jesus right now."],[1936.05,"That's gonna it's going to come to fruition later."],[1942.85,"You will say to me then, why does he still find fault for who has resisted his will?"],[1950.26,"So he's saying, you know, uh, well then, you know, how can he how can he condemn these people if this is part of his purpose?"],[1959.8,"If he's gonna bring good out of this uh out of this wickedness, well then why is this wickedness so wicked?"],[1966.61,"This is a question we ask about like Judas."],[1969.01,"Yeah. Well, didn't something good come out of it?"],[1972.33,"Or, you know, Joseph says, you know, to his brothers that them trying to murder him and then selling him into slavery, he's like, Well, God meant it, meant it to be for good."],[1981.05,"So does that justify what they did?"],[1982.65,"Oh, well, it's okay that you tried to murder your brother and sold him into slavery, because God brought good out."],[1989.71,"St. Paul is going to say no."],[1994.16,"Says, but indeed, oh man, who are you to reply against God?"],[1997.52,"Will a thing formed say to him who formed it, Why have you made me like this?"],[2002.72,"Does not the potter have power over the clay from the same lump to make one vessel for honor and another for dishonor?"],[2012.16,"Okay. So he's going to continue this example."],[2015.2,"But the immediate example is you make one vessel for honor and another for dishonor."],[2020.72,"What he's referring to here, uh, not to be overly graphic, is he's saying, a potter makes one pot to use to drink water out of, and he makes another one to use as a chamber pot."],[2032.21,"To go to the bathroom in it."],[2035.03,"And, you know, is the pot one pot going to complain?"],[2039.51,"Why do I have to be the one who can say?"],[2043.99,"I think uh but they're both they're both serving a purpose."],[2052.22,"They're both serving a purpose."],[2055.18,"Now he's going to continue."],[2057.42,"What if God, wanting to show his wrath and to make his power known, endured with much long suffering the vessels of wrath prepared for destruction?"],[2065.58,"And that he might make known the riches of his glory on the vessels of mercy, which he had prepared beforehand for glory, even us whom he called, not of the Jews only, but also. of the Gentiles"],[2078.5,"So, what's he referring to here when he talks about vessels of wrath?"],[2083.46,"Well, this language occurs back in the book of Jeremiah."],[2088.42,"And in the book of Jeremiah, where this language is used, Jeremiah speaking for God, is talking about the nations of Israel and Judah."],[2106.86,"Let's talk about the northern kingdom of Israel first."],[2110.3,"Okay. Ten tribes, including Manasseh, the most numerous tribe."],[2116.1,"They really tended to have."],[2117.62,"So by far the lion's share"],[2121.47,"Okay? Lion's share of Israel."],[2125.23,"Most of those tribes, like Dan, for example, if you read the book of Judges, was idolatrous from the beginning."],[2133.47,"From the beginning."],[2135.63,"The rest of them, as soon as they got their first king as a separate kingdom, Jeroboam, son of Nebat, sets up golden calves in Bethel and Dan and they all worship them."],[2145.31,"Sets up an apostate religion."],[2146.92,"At the beginning."],[2148.36,"Northern Kingdom of Israel existed for 200 years."],[2153.24,"All of it was just idolatry."],[2154.6,"I mean, Ahab and Jezebel, they're all worshiping Baal."],[2159.24,"They're all worshiping, they're all pagans, they're all wicked."],[2163.8,"And God wipes them off the face of the earth"],[2167.6,"That is the wrath at the end of 200 years."],[2171.76,"Why did they exist in the first place?"],[2180.16,"Why have those ten and a half tribes?"],[2184.32,"Why let them sit around and be wicked for 200 years?"],[2190.16,"Why give them the Torah if they were never going to follow it?"],[2197.44,"That's what St."],[2198.4,"Paul is paralleling this to."],[2202.34,"If these people weren't going to accept Christ, why do they even exist?"],[2206.6,"Why did they receive the Torah in the first place?"],[2209.12,"Why did they get the Old Testament part?"],[2211.04,"Why did they get that first half if they weren't going to accept the more important second half?"],[2217.7,"If they weren't going to accept it."],[2220.98,"And St. Paul is saying, look back at the old covenant."],[2227.96,"How many were called versus how many were chosen versus how many were faithful?"],[2232.97,"How many were brought out of Egypt?"],[2234.81,"First of all, that whole generation, except for two people, died in the wilderness."],[2238.49,"But how many were brought out of Egypt?"],[2241.61,"You know, their children went in the game."],[2243.05,"But how we were brought out of Egypt, and we'd ever and this is what this is what St."],[2246.89,"Paul is referring to"],[2253.09,"Right? So he says, God, makes his power known."],[2256.47,"He made his power known with Pharaoh."],[2259.41,"Why let Pharaoh exist?"],[2260.69,"Why let him be king?"],[2261.89,"Why? God used him to display his power to the world."],[2267.04,"And his wrath, his justice."],[2268.97,"His holiness. He did the same thing with Israel."],[2275.63,"Can Israel, the northern kingdom, can someone from could Jeroboam, son of Nebat, come to God and say, Hey, you know, why'd you make all these promises to me and give me the Torah if, you know, I wasn't gonna get anything?"],[2290.27,"Well, Jeroboam not will be talking to his golden calf."],[2295.83,"God actually said a prophet to him when he first became king and said, if you follow all my ways and keep all my commandments like David, I'll give you an everlasting dynasty just like his."],[2306.15,"And of course he didn't."],[2307.67,"So he didn't, but"],[2311.92,"So this the question St."],[2313.6,"Paul says is basically asking, Well, God, why did you do all these good things for me?"],[2319.78,"Why did you give me this life and let me do all these things if I'm just gonna be wicked and go to eternal condemnation?"],[2328.68,"So he shouldn't have even done those good things for you?"],[2331.24,"Is that what you're saying?"],[2334.0,"St. Paul is saying, God allows this."],[2337.34,"He deals patiently with these people for 200 years in the case of the northern kingdom of Israel, with all the people who were rejecting Jesus in his day, including St."],[2346.54,"Paul himself for a while."],[2348.86,"He's patient with them because he has a purpose."],[2352.7,"In some cases, that purpose may just be to show his power and his glory, and"],[2360.95,"And it and and in other cases, it may be something else that they're gonna come."],[2366.44,"And he says, but."],[2368.58,"He says, in any case, Judah saw what happened to Israel."],[2374.83,"Saw what happened to the North Kingdom."],[2375.47,"So there's one purpose"],[2380.85,"There's one purpose."],[2382.85,"Judah saw what comes of faithlessness."],[2387.31,"And so that's one purpose."],[2389.07,"There's going to be more, but that's one purpose that St."],[2391.47,"Paul mentions here."],[2394.03,"And he might make known the riches of his glory on the vessels of mercy which he had prepared beforehand for glory."],[2401.0,"He made those preparations beforehand, like we were talking about."],[2404.2,"So we see what comes of faithlessness."],[2409.18,"By God dealing patiently with the people who are faithless."],[2414.18,"Is that we see where that path leads."],[2419.48,"And this happens all the time on a very practical level in our lives."],[2425.64,"Speaking for myself, I have relatives who I look at as role models and people to be emulated, and I have relatives and family members who are sort of cautionary tales."],[2438.68,"You know, who have gone down the wrong path, and other people who know them, and other people look at that and say, okay, that's where that leads."],[2448.6,"That's not where I want to go."],[2451.34,"That's not where I want to go."],[2453.98,"And that's what St."],[2456.06,"Paul is appealing to here at a macro level."],[2459.6,"At a macro level."],[2463.0,"Verse 25 is He also says in Hosea, I will call them my people who are not my people, and her beloved who is not beloved."],[2470.36,"And it shall come to pass in the place where it was said to them, You are not my people, there they shall be called sons of the living God."],[2478.44,"Now, that was understood by a lot of the Jewish people to mean, oh, well, earlier in Hosea, God had said to his people, You are now Loa Me."],[2492.44,"You are now not my people."],[2493.24,"my people. He had the promise back then had been, I will be your God and you will be"],[2499.49,"You're not my people anymore."],[2501.25,"Why? Because he hadn't been their God for a long time."],[2504.55,"And so he finally says, You are not my people."],[2507.07,"And then, but then the prophecy in Hosea is that the day will come that in the same place."],[2512.29,"In the same land in Jerusalem, when I will say to those who are not my people, you are my people, and you are my beloved."],[2519.73,"They understood that to mean, oh, God's gonna come back to us Jewish people and accept us again."],[2528.26,"But Saint Paul understands this differently."],[2531.54,"Because as he just said, from both Jews and Gentiles, he understands this in terms of what happened at the Exodus."],[2538.5,"That God brought a people into being who didn't exist before."],[2541.72,"There was no nation of Israel before the Exodus."],[2544.92,"God creates a nation, He creates a nation."],[2549.44,"That's important because you notice he doesn't choose one of the seventy nations that already existed to be his nation and then throw out all the other ones."],[2558.4,"He creates a new one."],[2560.72,"He creates something new."],[2562.32,"In the same way, St."],[2563.6,"Paul is saying he's now created."],[2565.68,"He's now come back and created this new people."],[2567.76,"It happened in Jerusalem."],[2569.12,"It happened in Jesus Christ."],[2570.64,"Remember who's the firstborn."],[2572.64,"And it's through him that the inheritance is reckoned."],[2577.28,"Because Isaiah also cries out concerning Israel: Though the number of the children of Israel be as the sand of the sea, the remnant will be saved."],[2585.28,"For he will finish the work and cut it short in righteousness."],[2588.48,"Because the Lord will make a short work upon the earth."],[2591.93,"God isn't going to let this go on forever."],[2593.99,"He says, even though there are as many of them as the sands of the sea, the remnant."],[2599.4,"The remnant is going to be preserved."],[2602.04,"And this remnant, he's going to go on and describe it more, but this remnant is the core now of this new nation and this new people into which people from other nations, Gentiles, have been have been grafted in."],[2615.8,"And as Isaiah said before, unless the Lord of Sabaot had left us a seed, we would have become like Sodom and we would have been made like Gomorrah."],[2626.04,"So this is even this is even more specific, unless he left us a seed singular."],[2634.89,"So what's the difference between Judah and Israel?"],[2638.25,"Why does Judah still exist and Israel ceases to exist?"],[2642.73,"Well, because it's from Judah, from the tribe of Judah, that Jesus comes, who's the seed."],[2649.61,"Were it not for Christ, Israel would have been like Sodom and Gomorrah, meaning wiped from the face of the earth."],[2658.86,"So even in the old covenant, the preservation of the remnant was so that that remnant could produce Jesus."],[2667.27,"As the firstborn, as the one who inherits."],[2670.47,"So even that isn't a transition."],[2676.07,"But that it was for the sake of Jesus' birth."],[2679.75,"So I think this is probably, even though we're not quite at the end of the chapter, this is probably a good place to stop for the evening."],[2689.47,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2696.83,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2702.19,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.48,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:1-5 - Paul's grief for Israel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Transition at start of <b>ch. 9</b> - Paul moves to <b>new topic</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.1-2: \"I tell the truth in Christ\"; <b>two witnesses</b> - his <b>conscience</b> + the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Great sorrow, continual grief</b> in his heart</li><li class=\"d0\">v.3: would wish himself <b>accursed from Christ</b> for <b>his brethren</b></li><li class=\"d1\">willing to face <b>eternal condemnation</b>, cut off from Christ, if fellow <b>ethnic Jews / Judeans</b> could be saved</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>purest love</b> a human can have; other example: <b>Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses asks to be <b>blotted out of God's book</b> rather than God destroy Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Is it clear to Paul that <b>very few Israelites</b> follow Christ? <b>Yes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul himself once had <b>orders from the high priest</b>, going town to town killing Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">later <b>non-Christian Jews followed him</b> city to city trying to get him <b>stoned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Large segment <b>openly hostile</b> to the brethren everywhere</li><li class=\"d0\">His response: not to <b>hate them back</b> or \"they're all gonna go to hell\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>loves them so much</b> he'd go to hell for them</li><li class=\"d1\">parallel to <b>Christ loving and forgiving</b> those murdering him</li></ul>"},{"t":212.98,"lv":3,"h":"Israel's gifts culminate in the Messiah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recounts all they were given: <b>adoption, glory, covenants, law, service, promises, fathers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Culminates in <b>Jesus = their Messiah</b>, <b>son of David</b>, the <b>Jewish Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: \"I came to the <b>lost sheep of the tribes of Israel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">going to the <b>Gentiles came later</b>, after they'd rejected him</li><li class=\"d0\">v.5: Paul clearly identifies <b>Christ as God</b> - \"for anybody who might want to think otherwise\"</li></ul>"},{"t":279.62,"lv":2,"h":"The problem Paul is raising","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>introducing a problem</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If Jesus really is the <b>Messiah</b>, <b>son of David</b>, how God <b>visited his people to save them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why have so many Jews <b>rejected</b> him and show such <b>hatred</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">you'd expect them to <b>receive him joyfully</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Instead: <b>huge body of Jews</b> reject him</li><li class=\"d1\">and \"<b>weirdo pagans</b>\" with no Torah, priesthood, temple, sacrifices, prophets have <b>embraced him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"So this is weird. This is a problem\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul will address <b>how</b> and especially <b>why</b></li></ul>"},{"t":362.13,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:6-9 - Not all Israel is Israel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.6: \"not that the <b>word of God has taken no effect</b>\"; \"<b>not all Israel who are of Israel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Did <b>Torah, prophets, even Jesus' ministry</b> come to nothing? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not everyone <b>biologically descended from Abraham</b> was part of the <b>covenant people</b> from the beginning</li><li class=\"d1\">why he goes to <b>Isaac</b>: \"in Isaac your seed shall be called\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not new: it's <b>not</b> that everyone from Abraham was elect, then <b>Jesus came and everything changed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Always <b>faithful and unfaithful</b> in Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">often the unfaithful <b>greatly outnumbered</b> the faithful</li><li class=\"d0\">v.8-9: <b>children of the flesh</b> vs <b>children of the promise</b> - \"At this time I will come, and <b>Sarah shall have a son</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":499.81,"lv":3,"h":"Ishmael","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ishmael</b> was not the promised one; seed reckoned through <b>one</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leads up to <b>Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Does that mean Ishmael <b>went to hell</b>? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis: he was <b>blessed by God and made numerous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">promise to Abraham: <b>nations plural</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Line of promise</b> reckoned through <b>one branch</b>; faithful and unfaithful within each</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul is talking about <b>faithless Jews</b> and <b>faithful Gentiles</b> - both in view</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Ishmael is just a bad guy because he's not a Jew\" <b>doesn't work</b></li></ul>"},{"t":568.4,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:10-13 - Jacob and Esau","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rebecca conceived by Isaac; <b>before birth, before good or evil</b>: \"<b>the older shall serve the younger</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Jacob I have loved, Esau I have hated</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvinists</b> \"jump up and down\": before works, God loves one and hates the other</li><li class=\"d0\">Works \"unless you've read the book of <b>Genesis</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jacob I loved, Esau I hated\" is <b>not Genesis</b> - it's <b>Malachi 1:2-3</b>, after the <b>exile</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis quote = \"<b>older shall serve the younger</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":654.93,"lv":3,"h":"Firstborn status and the birthright","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Twins</b> - Esau out a couple seconds first = <b>firstborn</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Firstborn would inherit promises from Abraham: the <b>land of Canaan</b>, the promise to be <b>his God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then distribute to his brothers - \"how firstborn status works\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God had a purpose: chose <b>Jacob (Israel)</b> as firstborn</li><li class=\"d0\">Esau: hungry, traded <b>birthright for a bowl of stew</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fits ch. 8: <b>following the flesh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>faithlessness</b> - if he believed the promise of that land, <b>would he sell it for soup?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"He didn't really value it... didn't have faith\"</li></ul>"},{"t":791.0,"lv":3,"h":"Esau's fate in Genesis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Calvinist reading: Esau <b>evil, goes to hell</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis shows the <b>exact opposite</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob returns, fears Esau will <b>kill him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sends <b>wives, servants, kids with gifts</b> ahead - \"<b>butter him up</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"look at all these <b>cute kids</b>... they won't have a dad\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Esau <b>throws his arms around him and weeps</b>, <b>forgives and loves</b> him</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not a faithless, wicked person\" - not how the Bible presents him</li><li class=\"d0\">But he's <b>not the firstborn</b></li></ul>"},{"t":883.31,"lv":3,"h":"The Edomites in Deuteronomy 2","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nation descended from him: <b>Edom / Edomites</b> - Malachi quote is about them, much later</li><li class=\"d0\">Were they <b>just bad from the start</b>? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deut 2</b>: Israel on way to the land after 40 years; reach <b>Edom</b> in the south</li><li class=\"d1\">Edom is <b>part of the land promised to Abraham</b>, \"small part\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God: not to take \"<b>one foot of land</b>\" - \"<b>they're your brothers</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I brought them to this land... <b>drove out the giant clans</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can <b>trade, visit</b>, but <b>no arms</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So they got <b>part of Abraham's inheritance</b> - \"not wicked evil yet\"</li></ul>"},{"t":992.72,"lv":3,"h":"Midianites, Jethro, Job","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Moses</b> flees Egypt after murdering the Egyptian → stays with <b>Midianites</b></li><li class=\"d1\">descended from <b>Esau's grandson Midian</b>; tribes = mostly <b>Edomites</b> + a few <b>Ishmaelites</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Father-in-law <b>Jethro</b> = <b>priest of God Most High</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at the <b>mountain of God</b> where Moses learns the name <b>Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">already being worshiped by the Edomites</li><li class=\"d0\">Whole time in Egypt, descendants of <b>Ishmael and Esau</b> worshiping <b>Yahweh</b> in the desert</li><li class=\"d0\">Archaeology: earliest references to <b>Yahweh</b> = <b>11th-12th c. BC Egyptian documents</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"god of the <b>Midianite tribes</b>\"; <b>no mention of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Septuagint</b>: end of <b>Job</b> identifies Job as <b>Jobab, Esau's great-grandson</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So Edomites in Torah: <b>not wicked</b> - \"your brothers\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1124.05,"lv":3,"h":"Why Malachi condemns Edom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why hated at end of the exile? Read <b>Obadiah</b> + context of <b>Malachi</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When <b>Babylonians took Judah into exile</b>, Edomites</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>refused to help</b> against Babylon, <b>refused to help refugees</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>robbed</b> Judahites being taken, <b>mocked and scoffed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used it to <b>take territory in Judah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Condemned for <b>victimizing their brothers</b>; unfriendly to those who returned</li><li class=\"d0\">Famous Edomite in the Bible: <b>Herod</b> - <b>Edomite → Idumean</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1211.9,"lv":2,"h":"Firstborn status, not salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ishmael and Esau are <b>not</b> \"faithful, saved\" vs \"wicked, unsaved\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>one line</b> through which inheritance / <b>firstborn</b> is reckoned</li><li class=\"d0\">Where does it end up? Ch. 8 → <b>Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"always been one firstborn. Now that firstborn is <b>Jesus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To be an <b>heir</b> you must be <b>co-heir with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as before, co-heir with <b>Isaac</b>, with <b>Jacob</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ = firstborn <b>par excellence</b>, <b>inherits all creation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Descended from Abraham but <b>reject the firstborn</b> → <b>won't inherit</b> apart from him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Edomites</b>: early on <b>co-heirs</b> via relation to Israel/Judah</li><li class=\"d1\">turned against Judah → <b>lost inheritance</b>, <b>cut off</b> though still biologically Abraham's</li><li class=\"d0\">Same now: Jews cut off by <b>rejecting Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not about <b>salvation</b>, but about <b>firstborn status</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1374.61,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:14-16 - Is God unrighteous?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.14: \"Is there <b>unrighteousness with God</b>? <b>Certainly not</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't God make promises that now won't be kept?</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Certainly not\" = Greek <b>optative</b> mood</li><li class=\"d1\">used <b>64 times</b> in the Bible; ~<b>85%</b> are <b>Paul</b> - \"may it never happen\"</li><li class=\"d1\">one of Paul's <b>verbal tics</b>, like any teacher</li><li class=\"d0\">v.15-16: \"I will have mercy on whom I will have mercy\" (to <b>Moses</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not of him who <b>wills</b> nor of him who <b>runs</b>, but of God who shows mercy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God under <b>no obligation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">charge of unrighteousness assumes God <b>owes</b> salvation to all Abraham's seed</li><li class=\"d0\">He acts <b>freely, out of love</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing we do <b>obligates</b> God: \"descended from Abraham, <b>circumcised</b>, wore <b>tassels</b>, <b>no pork</b>\" → <b>No</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1531.2,"lv":3,"h":"Romans 9:17-18 - Pharaoh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"For this very purpose I <b>raised you up</b>... <b>my name declared in all the earth</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Has mercy on whom he wills, whom he wills <b>he hardens</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">In Exodus: sometimes <b>Pharaoh's heart was hardened</b>, sometimes <b>Pharaoh hardened his heart</b>, sometimes <b>God hardened</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">mixed together, no clear rhyme or reason</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: God <b>using Pharaoh</b> to accomplish something in the <b>plagues</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1631.75,"lv":3,"h":"Exodus 1 - what Pharaoh had done","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Exodus 1: Israel <b>fruitful and multiplying</b> - language from <b>Genesis</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God's command to <b>Adam and Eve</b>, again after expulsion, again to <b>Noah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same word as <b>teeming</b> in Gen 1 (birds, fish) - \"this is what God does, he <b>brings life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharaoh: \"pretty soon more of them than us\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tells the <b>midwives</b>: determine <b>male or female</b> (Egyptians had ways), <b>abort</b> males</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>infant upon the potter's wheel</b>\" - Egyptian god <b>Khnum</b>, ram's head, forms humans on a <b>potter's wheel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Midwives refuse; excuse: Israelite women are <b>hardy</b>, \"<b>woman up</b>\" - baby born before we arrive</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharaoh: throw every male baby in the <b>river</b> → <b>untold thousands</b> massacred</li></ul>"},{"t":1789.17,"lv":3,"h":"Why the plagues, why harden","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Plagues build to <b>Passover</b> and death of <b>Egypt's firstborn</b> - God \"<b>balancing the scales</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Display that the <b>gods of Egypt</b> can't withstand him</li><li class=\"d1\">judgment on the gods; <b>Pharaoh</b> thought himself a god</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no power of life and death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Egypt = most powerful nation → display to <b>everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rahab</b> to the spies: \"we heard what your God did to the Egyptians\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharaoh <b>couldn't chicken out halfway</b> - \"in for a penny, in for a pound\"</li><li class=\"d0\">For God's end goal, <b>Pharaoh's heart hardened for a time</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1882.77,"lv":3,"h":"The comparison to unbelieving Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why important: comparison to <b>Jews who haven't accepted Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>accidental</b> - <b>God has a purpose</b> in this</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Dropping the dime\": purpose in them <b>not accepting now</b></li><li class=\"d1\">comes to <b>fruition later</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1942.85,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:19-21 - The potter and the clay","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Objection: \"Why does he still <b>find fault</b>? Who has resisted his will?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if God brings <b>good out of wickedness</b>, why is it so wicked?</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Judas</b>; like <b>Joseph's brothers</b> - \"God meant it for good\"</li><li class=\"d1\">does that justify <b>trying to murder</b> him and selling him? </li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>No</b> - \"who are you, O man, to reply against God?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Does not the <b>potter</b> have power over the clay... one vessel for <b>honor</b>, another for <b>dishonor</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pot to <b>drink water</b> from vs a <b>chamber pot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">will one complain? \"both serving a <b>purpose</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2057.42,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:22-24 - Vessels of wrath and mercy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"What if God, wanting to show his <b>wrath</b>... endured with <b>much long suffering</b> the <b>vessels of wrath</b> prepared for destruction\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To make known <b>riches of his glory</b> on <b>vessels of mercy</b>, prepared beforehand</li><li class=\"d1\">\"even us whom he called, <b>not of the Jews only, but also of the Gentiles</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2083.46,"lv":3,"h":"Jeremiah's vessels - the northern kingdom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Language from <b>Jeremiah</b>, speaking of <b>Israel and Judah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Northern kingdom</b>: <b>ten tribes</b> incl. <b>Manasseh</b>, \"lion's share\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Dan</b> idolatrous from the start (Judges)</li><li class=\"d1\">first king <b>Jeroboam son of Nebat</b>: <b>golden calves</b> at <b>Bethel and Dan</b>, apostate religion</li><li class=\"d1\">lasted <b>200 years</b>, all <b>idolatry</b> - <b>Ahab and Jezebel</b>, <b>Baal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>wipes them off the face of the earth</b> = the <b>wrath</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Questions: why did they exist? why let them be wicked <b>200 years</b>? why give the <b>Torah</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's parallel: if they won't accept <b>Christ</b>, why exist, why get the <b>Old Testament</b> \"first half\"?</li><li class=\"d0\">Look back at old covenant: <b>called vs chosen vs faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">generation out of Egypt <b>died in the wilderness</b> except two</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharaoh: God used him to display <b>power, wrath, justice, holiness</b>; same with Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Jeroboam could hardly complain - prophet promised him a <b>dynasty like David's</b> if faithful; <b>he didn't</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2337.34,"lv":3,"h":"God's patience and the purpose in it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Imagined complaint: \"why did you do good things for me if I'd be <b>wicked</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>deals patiently</b> with them</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>200 years</b> for the northern kingdom</li><li class=\"d1\">people rejecting Jesus in his day, incl. <b>Paul himself</b> for a while</li><li class=\"d0\">Purposes: sometimes <b>show power and glory</b>; other cases something else</li><li class=\"d0\">One purpose: <b>Judah saw what happened to Israel</b> - <b>what comes of faithlessness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">By his patience we <b>see where that path leads</b></li><li class=\"d1\">practical: <b>relatives as role models</b> and others as <b>cautionary tales</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's not where I want to go\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul appeals to this at a <b>macro level</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2463.0,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:25-26 - Hosea","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I will call them <b>my people who are not my people</b>... <b>sons of the living God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Many Jews read it: earlier God told them <b>Lo-Ammi</b> = \"not my people\" → he'll come back and accept <b>Jews</b> again</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not my people\" because he <b>hadn't been their God for a long time</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophecy: in the <b>same place</b> he'll say \"you are my people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul reads differently: <b>Jews and Gentiles</b>, in terms of the <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>brought a people into being</b> who didn't exist; <b>no Israel before the Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't pick <b>one of seventy nations</b> and discard the rest - <b>created a new one</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same now: <b>new people</b>, in <b>Jerusalem</b>, in <b>Jesus Christ</b> - the <b>firstborn</b></li><li class=\"d1\">inheritance reckoned through <b>him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2577.28,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:27-29 - Isaiah and the remnant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Though the number of Israel be as the <b>sand of the sea</b>, the <b>remnant</b> will be saved\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>make a short work upon the earth</b>\" - God won't let it go on forever</li><li class=\"d0\">Remnant = <b>core of the new nation</b>; Gentiles <b>grafted in</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Isaiah: \"unless the <b>Lord of Sabaoth</b> left us a <b>seed</b>, we'd be like <b>Sodom and Gomorrah</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>seed singular</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why does <b>Judah</b> still exist, <b>Israel</b> ceases? From <b>Judah</b> comes <b>Jesus</b>, the seed</li><li class=\"d1\">without Christ, Israel <b>wiped out</b> like Sodom and Gomorrah</li><li class=\"d0\">Even in old covenant, remnant preserved <b>so it could produce Jesus</b>, firstborn and heir</li><li class=\"d0\">Not quite end of chapter - \"good place to stop for the evening\"</li></ul>"}]});
